## Step 2 — Semantic Distinguishability

Since `inclusive_scan_kernel` is never called anywhere in the code path, the mutation in that kernel has no effect on the output. The actual computation is done by `row_scan_kernel`, which is unchanged between the original and mutated versions.

Looking at `row_scan_kernel`:
```cpp
template<typename T>
__global__ void row_scan_kernel(const T* input, T* output, int rows, int cols) {
    int row = blockIdx.x;
    int tid = threadIdx.x;

    if (row >= rows) return;

    extern __shared__ T shared[];

    // Load row into shared memory
    for (int i = tid; i < cols; i += blockDim.x) {
        shared[i] = input[row * cols + i];
    }
    __syncthreads();

    // Perform sequential scan in shared memory
    if (tid == 0) {
        for (int i = 1; i < cols; i++) {
            shared[i] += shared[i - 1];
        }
    }
    __syncthreads();

    // Store results
    for (int i = tid; i < cols; i += blockDim.x) {
        output[row * cols + i] = shared[i];
    }
}
```

This kernel performs the actual inclusive scan operation by loading data into shared memory, computing cumulative sums sequentially, and writing results back. Since this kernel remains identical in both versions, the program behavior is unchanged regardless of the mutation in the unreachable `inclusive_scan_kernel`.

The mutated code never executes because the kernel launch is hardcoded to call `row_scan_kernel`. No test input can trigger execution of the modified kernel since the function call is fixed at compile time. The mutation exists in dead code that's never reached during program execution, making it impossible for any test case to detect the difference between the original and mutated versions.
